\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {84}{\ignorespaces What each optimization bought, per map pass, for \texttt  {PiecewiseFunctions} (percent; companion to Table~\ref {tab:pldi-map-PiecewiseFunctions}). Every column is (\emph  {baseline} $-$ \emph  {feature enabled}) / \emph  {feature enabled}, which is identically $({speedup} - 1) \times 100$: $+100\%$ means the feature made the pass twice as fast, $+700\%$ eight times as fast, and a negative value means it made the pass slower. Percentages are scale-free, so they are comparable across passes whose absolute times differ widely. See Table~\ref {tab:pldi-delta-legend} for each column's exact definition. `--' marks a pass where either side of the difference was not measured; the timing table above says which.}}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pldi-map-delta-PiecewiseFunctions}{{84}{18}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {85}{\ignorespaces Integer-width high-arithmetic-intensity vectorization. Every configuration was compiled for the same SIMD target, 256-bit AVX2 (\texttt  {-mavx2}); Gibbon's vectorizer and the C compiler's auto-vectorizer both target it, so the columns are like-for-like. Every column is compiled with the C compiler's auto-vectorizer disabled (\texttt  {--no-gcc-vectorize}, which suppresses basic-block/SLP vectorization as well as loop vectorization), so the SIMD column isolates Gibbon's own vectorizer rather than the backend's; absolute times are correspondingly higher than a default build. Selective buffer sharing is enabled from the \textbf  {SoA loopify shared} column onwards, including the SIMD column, so those cells are directly comparable with the per-program map tables, whose SoA columns also enable it. \textbf  {SIMD spd.} divides the shared column by the SIMD one -- the two differ by the vectorizer alone, so it reports what vectorization buys and nothing else; \textbf  {AoS-vs-SIMD} keeps AoS as its baseline and therefore includes every SoA-side optimization. Int64's packed multiply is EMULATED, and its SIMD column is compiled with \texttt  {--simd-w64-multiply} to measure it. x86 has no packed 64-bit multiply below AVX-512DQ, so the helper synthesizes one from three packed $32{\times }32{\to }64$ multiplies plus two shifts and two adds, all in registers -- correct SIMD, but seven instructions for two lanes against one \texttt  {imul} per lane scalar. The compiler therefore refuses it by default; the number here is what enabling it costs, and a speedup below $1{\times }$ in that row is the expected result rather than a defect.}}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:arith_intensity}{{85}{18}{}{}{}}
\gdef \@abspage@last{18}
